"title":232,"path":233,"stem":234,"icon":235},"CRI Format","\u002Fplatform\u002Freference\u002Fcri-format","04.platform\u002F12.reference\u002F01.cri-format","i-lucide-link",{"title":237,"path":238,"stem":239,"icon":240},"Grind Jobs","\u002Fplatform\u002Freference\u002Fgrind-jobs","04.platform\u002F12.reference\u002F02.grind-jobs","i-lucide-workflow",{"title":242,"path":243,"stem":244,"icon":179},"Project Publishing Design","\u002Fplatform\u002Freference\u002Fproject-publishing-design","04.platform\u002F12.reference\u002F03.project-publishing-design",{"title":246,"path":247,"stem":248,"icon":38},"Sign-In Design","\u002Fplatform\u002Freference\u002Fsign-in-design","04.platform\u002F12.reference\u002F04.sign-in-design",{"title":250,"path":251,"stem":252,"icon":253},"Development Server","\u002Fplatform\u002Fdevelopment-server","04.platform\u002F13.development-server","i-lucide-server-cog",{"title":255,"path":256,"stem":257,"icon":258},"Supply Chain Security","\u002Fplatform\u002Fsupply-chain-security","04.platform\u002F14.supply-chain-security","i-lucide-package-check",{"title":260,"path":261,"stem":262,"icon":263},"Enterprise License","\u002Fplatform\u002Fenterprise-license","04.platform\u002F15.enterprise-license","i-lucide-badge-check",{"id":265,"title":64,"body":266,"description":553,"extension":554,"links":555,"meta":556,"navigation":557,"path":65,"seo":558,"stem":66,"__hash__":559},"docs\u002F03.apps\u002F03.application-structure\u002F03.artifact-types.md",{"type":267,"value":268,"toc":543},"minimark",[269,278,282,285,290,314,319,330,333,337,344,348,356,360,375,378,382,385,389,397,401,409,413,416,420,425,429,434,438,441,445,450,454,462,466,469,540],[270,271,272,273,277],"p",{},"Every project in a Kinotic application contributes one or more ",[274,275,276],"strong",{},"artifacts",". An artifact is a typed output that the platform knows how to build, deploy, and manage. This page describes each artifact type and when to use it.",[279,280,89],"h2",{"id":281},"persistence",[270,283,284],{},"The most common starting point. A persistence artifact defines your domain models (entities) and gets auto-generated CRUD services in return.",[270,286,287],{},[274,288,289],{},"What you provide:",[291,292,293],"ul",{},[294,295,296,297,301,302,305,306,309,310,313],"li",{},"Entity classes decorated with ",[298,299,300],"code",{},"@Entity"," and field-level annotations like ",[298,303,304],{},"@NotNull",", ",[298,307,308],{},"@Precision",", and ",[298,311,312],{},"@AutoGeneratedId",".",[270,315,316],{},[274,317,318],{},"What the platform provides:",[291,320,321,324,327],{},[294,322,323],{},"Full CRUD operations (save, find, delete) with pagination and sorting.",[294,325,326],{},"Named queries derived from method naming conventions.",[294,328,329],{},"Automatic storage provisioning and indexing.",[270,331,332],{},"Persistence is typically the first artifact you create because it establishes the data model that the rest of the application builds on.",[279,334,336],{"id":335},"microservice","Microservice",[270,338,339,340,313],{},"A microservice artifact is long-running code that executes in a pod or VM. It publishes services that other parts of the application can discover and call through ",[341,342,343],"a",{"href":82},"service proxies",[270,345,346],{},[274,347,289],{},[291,349,350,353],{},[294,351,352],{},"TypeScript classes that implement your business logic.",[294,354,355],{},"Service interfaces that you want to expose to the rest of the application.",[270,357,358],{},[274,359,318],{},[291,361,362,365,372],{},[294,363,364],{},"Automatic service registration in the Service Directory.",[294,366,367,368,371],{},"Service discovery and zone-based routing so other projects can call your service through a ",[341,369,370],{"href":82},"service proxy"," with a simple function call.",[294,373,374],{},"Lifecycle management, scaling, and health monitoring.",[270,376,377],{},"Use a microservice when your logic goes beyond simple CRUD -- orchestrating across multiple entities, calling external APIs, or running long-lived workflows.",[279,379,381],{"id":380},"batch-job","Batch Job",[270,383,384],{},"A batch job artifact is code that runs on a schedule. It is ideal for periodic tasks like data aggregation, report generation, cleanup routines, or syncing with external systems.",[270,386,387],{},[274,388,289],{},[291,390,391,394],{},[294,392,393],{},"TypeScript code that performs the job.",[294,395,396],{},"A schedule definition (cron expression or interval).",[270,398,399],{},[274,400,318],{},[291,402,403,406],{},[294,404,405],{},"Scheduled execution with retry and failure handling.",[294,407,408],{},"Logging and observability for each run.",[279,410,412],{"id":411},"ui-component","UI Component",[270,414,415],{},"A UI component artifact produces reusable building blocks that can be shared across multiple frontends within the application. Think of it as an internal component library.",[270,417,418],{},[274,419,289],{},[291,421,422],{},[294,423,424],{},"Reusable UI components packaged for consumption by frontend projects.",[270,426,427],{},[274,428,318],{},[291,430,431],{},[294,432,433],{},"Versioning and distribution within the application.",[279,435,437],{"id":436},"frontend","Frontend",[270,439,440],{},"A frontend artifact is a complete user interface that gets deployed as a static site. It consumes services published by other projects in the application.",[270,442,443],{},[274,444,289],{},[291,446,447],{},[294,448,449],{},"A web application (built with any framework) that compiles to static assets.",[270,451,452],{},[274,453,318],{},[291,455,456,459],{},[294,457,458],{},"Static hosting with CDN distribution.",[294,460,461],{},"Automatic connection to the application's services through the Kinotic client libraries.",[279,463,465],{"id":464},"combining-artifact-types","Combining Artifact Types",[270,467,468],{},"Most real-world applications combine several artifact types. A typical setup might look like this:",[470,471,472,488],"table",{},[473,474,475],"thead",{},[476,477,478,482,485],"tr",{},[479,480,481],"th",{},"Project",[479,483,484],{},"Artifact Type",[479,486,487],{},"Role",[489,490,491,504,516,528],"tbody",{},[476,492,493,499,501],{},[494,495,496],"td",{},[298,497,498],{},"data",[494,500,89],{},[494,502,503],{},"Defines entities and provides auto-generated CRUD services",[476,505,506,511,513],{},[494,507,508],{},[298,509,510],{},"services",[494,512,336],{},[494,514,515],{},"Implements business rules that orchestrate across entities",[476,517,518,523,525],{},[494,519,520],{},[298,521,522],{},"jobs",[494,524,381],{},[494,526,527],{},"Runs nightly data aggregation",[476,529,530,535,537],{},[494,531,532],{},[298,533,534],{},"web",[494,536,437],{},[494,538,539],{},"The main user interface",[270,541,542],{},"Each project is independently developed and versioned, but they all deploy as part of the same application and communicate through the Service Directory.",{"title":544,"searchDepth":545,"depth":545,"links":546},"",2,[547,548,549,550,551,552],{"id":281,"depth":545,"text":89},{"id":335,"depth":545,"text":336},{"id":380,"depth":545,"text":381},{"id":411,"depth":545,"text":412},{"id":436,"depth":545,"text":437},{"id":464,"depth":545,"text":465},"Learn about the different artifact types a Kinotic project can contribute -- from persistence and microservices to frontends.","md",null,{},true,{"title":64,"description":553},"1BaBm8041RP9SKrZbDluR-X4vi3Hvd-ND6RsIAx_opg",[561,563],{"title":60,"path":61,"stem":62,"description":562,"children":-1},"Learn how to configure Kinotic applications and the projects that compose them.",{"title":31,"path":74,"stem":75,"description":564,"children":-1},"How services work in Kinotic and the communication patterns they support.",1792981940288]